\begin{proposition}[The one-player half of \Cref{thm:seed-dichotomy} fails]\label{audhyb:dichotomy-counterexample}
Let $G:=WD(4,2,6)$ of \Cref{def:wedge}: $N=21$, stopping, $\Vmin=\emptyset$, $w^\ast(v_i)=w^\ast(a_{i,1})=\tfrac12$ and $w^\ast(b_i)=\tfrac{31}{64}$. Let $\sigma_0$ be the profile $v_i\mapsto b_i$. Then $\val_{\sigma_0}(v_i)=\tfrac7{16}$ and $\val_{\sigma_0}(a_{i,1})=\tfrac{113}{256}$, the greedy $\tau$ is vacuous, and $M(0,0)$ has $L=\val_{\sigma_0}$, $U=\val^{\tau}=w^\ast$, so that
\[
 \Delta^{L,U}_0(v_1,a_{1,1})=\tfrac{15}{256},\quad
 \Delta^{L,U}_0(a_{1,1},v_1)=\tfrac1{16},\quad
 \Delta^{L,U}_0(v_1,b_1)=\tfrac1{16},\quad
 \Delta^{L,U}_0(b_1,v_1)=\tfrac3{64},
\]
and the pair entries are $\Delta_0(b_1,a_{1,1})=\tfrac{11}{256}$ and $\Delta_0(a_{1,1},b_1)=\tfrac1{16}$. All six are strictly positive, and the same holds at $v_2$ by the index automorphism and on $WD(6,3,7)$. Hence $M(0,0)$ decides neither value-distinguishing Max vertex, although one of $\Vmax,\Vmin$ is empty.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Exact rational computation; $w^\ast$ and $\val_{\sigma_0}$ by enumeration of positional pairs (nothing to enumerate on the Min side), $\val^\tau$ likewise.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{audhyb:dichotomy-repair}
The gap is in the last clause of the proof of \Cref{thm:seed-dichotomy}: with $\Vmin=\emptyset$ the test $TU=U$ identifies $U$ with $w^\ast$, but $L=\val_{\sigma_p}$ is identified with nothing, so $\Delta_0=U-L\ne D$. The other half is sound: with $\Vmax=\emptyset$ the profile $\sigma$ is vacuous, $\val_\sigma=w^\ast$, and the first part of the theorem gives $\val^\tau=w^\ast$, so $\Delta_0=D$. Either restrict the clause to $\Vmax=\emptyset$, or let $M$ set $L:=U$ once the $O(N)$ test $TU=U$ has succeeded. The residue of \Cref{rem:frontier-moved} --- a stalling instance of $M(p,k)$ is a stopping SSG on which switch-all improvement has not converged after $p$ rounds --- follows from the theorem's first sufficient condition alone and is untouched. What must be withdrawn is ``every instance on which $M(p,k)$ stalls has both players present'', and with it the inference in \Cref{rem:wedge} that $WD$ is decided at slack round zero \emph{because} it is one-player. That conclusion survives for $p\ge1$: from each of the four profiles of $WD(4,2,6)$ and of $WD(6,3,7)$ one all-switches round reaches $\Opt$, and $M(1,0)$ then fires at both Max vertices in both directions (i) and (ii).
\end{remark}

