\begin{lemma}[Closing the diagonal case in \Cref{thm:matching-barrier}]\label{audhyb:diagonal}
\Cref{def:lmc} permits a base equal to $(Z_0\times V)\cup(V\times Z_1)$ with no diagonal, so the step ``each in the base or on the diagonal'' at $(m,k)$ in $K_{\#}$ requires an argument; here it is. Let $A\subseteq\Pi$ be a clause deriving $(5,1)$ in $G_8$. If $(x^{(1)},y)\notin A$ then the atoms of $A$ interpret at $(m,k)$ among $(m,h),(g,h),(k,h)$, all in $V\times Z_1$, so $(m,k)$ is derived. If $(x^{(1)},y)\in A$ then, its interpretation at $(5,1)$ being $(1,1)$, the calculus derives $(1,1)$ in $G_8$ by some clause $B$ assigned to $\mathrm{frame}(1,1)$, and soundness confines $B$ to the value-true atoms $\{(x,y^{(0)}),(x^{(0)},y^{(0)}),(x^{(1)},y),(x^{(1)},y^{(0)}),(x^{(1)},y^{(1)})\}$. But $\mathrm{frame}(k,k)=\mathrm{frame}(1,1)$: both have types $(\mathrm{avg},\mathrm{avg},t_0,\mathrm{avg},\mathrm{avg},t_0)$ and equality pattern $\{x=y,\ x^{(0)}=y^{(0)},\ x^{(1)}=y^{(1)}\}$. At $(k,k)$ those five atoms interpret as $(k,h),(h,h),(t_0,k),(t_0,h),(t_0,t_0)$, all in $(Z_0\times V)\cup(V\times Z_1)$ since $h\in Z_1$ and $t_0\in Z_0$. So $(k,k)$ is derived, hence so is $(m,k)$, and $w^\ast(m)=1>\tfrac12=w^\ast(k)$ contradicts soundness in either reading. Finally a transitive chain $4=v_0\preceq\cdots\preceq v_n=1$ has a least index $i$ with $v_i=1$, and then $v_{i-1}\in\{4,5\}$, so the two links $(4,1)$ and $(5,1)$ already excluded suffice.
\end{lemma}
